      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Preston was a frontier town perched on a high bluff in the Washita Bend of the Red River, near today’s Pottsboro.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/preston-tx-grayson-county",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1870 the original town was largely abandoned, and a new Preston community emerged more centrally within the bend.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/preston-tx-grayson-county",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Missouri, Kansas and Texas railroad crossed the Red River east of Preston in 1871, bypassing the town and sparking its decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preston%2C_Texas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
